// `char` is one Unicode CODEPOINT, not a byte and not a number. AGENTS.md opens with the story of
// SPEC.md claiming the byte/codepoint distinction was enforced when it was not — and the
// codepoint/INTEGER distinction was in the same state until 0.9.104, for a mechanical reason: `char`
// lowered straight to `uint32_t`, so by the time any rule ran there was nothing left to compare. It was
// never exempted; it was invisible.
//
// Both directions, and the `==` that reads as a comparison of two different types and is not.
// `lib/std/ascii/ascii.kama` has always written `cast<uint32>(c)` on every line — this is the rule that
// discipline was already following.
fn int32 main()
{
    char c = 'a';
    uint32 u = 65ui32;
    uint32 n = c;
    char d = u;
    bool same = (c == u);
    return 0;
}
